For $A\in M_n(F)$, the following are equivalent:

1. $A$ is invertible;
2. $x\mapsto Ax$ is a linear isomorphism;
3. $N(A)=\{0\}$;
4. $\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$;
5. $A$ has a pivot in every row and every column;
6. the RREF of $A$ is $I_n$;
7. for every $b\in F^n$, the equation $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution.

Let $S$ be the Smith-Volterra-Cantor set ([[def-fat-cantor-set]]). Then:

1. $S$ is closed and bounded, hence compact
   ([[thm-heine-borel-characterisation-r]]);
2. $S$ is perfect ([[def-perfect-set-r]]);
3. $S$ is nowhere dense ([[def-nowhere-dense-meager]]);
4. if $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ are sequences of reals with $a_k \le b_k$,
   $S \subseteq \bigcup_k [a_k,b_k]$ and $\sum_{k<i}(b_k - a_k) \le M$ for every
   $i \in \mathbb{N}$, then $M \ge 2^{-1}$.

Each of the following families generates $\mathcal B(\mathbb R)$:

1. all open subsets of $\mathbb R$;
2. all closed subsets of $\mathbb R$;
3. all open intervals $(a,b)$ with $a<b$;
4. all rational open intervals $(p,q)$ with $p,q\in\mathbb Q$ and $p<q$;
5. all half-open intervals $(a,b]$ with $a<b$;
6. all open right rays $(a,\infty)$;
7. all rational open right rays $(q,\infty)$ with $q\in\mathbb Q$.

